# Everything an install writes into this fixture is ignored, on purpose.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The committed state of this directory is a clean Next.js 16 app: a layout, a
# home page, a stylesheet, and configs. Nothing else. CI takes it from there,
# runs `shadcn add` and the CLI against it, and asserts what those two produced.
#
# If any of the generated output were committed, the pipeline would still be
# green and would still prove nothing:
#
#   * `shadcn add --overwrite` would be rewriting files that already exist, so
#     "the registry installs into a clean project" would never actually be
#     tested. The first real consumer would be the first clean install.
#   * The idempotency gate stages the tree (`git add -A apps/fixture-next16`),
#     runs `agentblog init` a second time, then reads `git diff --exit-code`.
#     What that has to catch is a patcher rewriting a file it already patched, so
#     the diff should cover exactly the files the CLI edits: next.config.ts,
#     app/layout.tsx, components.json, package.json. Install output is copied
#     verbatim from the registry and is idempotent by construction, so leaving it
#     tracked adds a few hundred lines of noise a reviewer has to read past to
#     find the one line that matters.
#   * Anyone who ran the install locally to debug something would be one `git
#     add -A` away from committing a half-patched fixture, and it would look
#     like ordinary work in review.
#
# Keep this list in step with the `target` fields in
# apps/web/registry/blog/registry.json and apps/web/registry/agent/registry.json.
# A new registry file with an untracked target lands here as an untracked file
# and turns the idempotency diff red for no real reason.

# Build output and dependencies. node_modules is restated rather than left to the
# root .gitignore because `pnpm clean-install` in this package excludes it by
# name, and the two lists should be readable side by side.
.next/
node_modules/
next-env.d.ts
*.tsbuildinfo

# Written by `shadcn add @agentblog/blog`. `app/` and `lib/` are listed file by
# file rather than wholesale, because this fixture owns app/layout.tsx,
# app/page.tsx, app/globals.css and lib/utils.ts and those must stay tracked.
agentblog.config.ts
app/api/
app/authors/
app/blog/
app/editorial-policy/
app/feed.xml/
app/llms.txt/
app/not-found.tsx
app/robots.ts
app/sitemap.ts
components/
content/
hooks/
lib/*
!lib/utils.ts
styles/

# Written by `shadcn add @agentblog/agent-kit`.
.claude/
AGENTS.agentblog.md

# Written by `agentblog init` and `agentblog doctor --fix`. AGENTS.md is the
# file scripts/assert-agents-md.mjs inspects, and CLAUDE.md is the file it
# asserts we never wrote. Both are generated per run, so neither is committed.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.agentblog/

# INDEXNOW_KEY and AGENTBLOG_REVALIDATE_SECRET. Secrets, and CI generates them.
.env
.env.local
.env*.local

# The IndexNow key file that `agentblog doctor --fix` generates. Its name is
# random per install, so committing one would make the idempotency diff in CI
# depend on which run produced it.
public/*.txt
